      "text": "To solve this problem, let's first denote the four positive integers by \\( a, b, c, d \\). According to the problem, when these integers are divided by 11, the remainders are 2, 4, 6, and 8, respectively. This gives us the following congruences:\n\\[ a \\equiv 2 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ b \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ d \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the remainder when the sum of these four integers \\( a + b + c + d \\) is divided by 11. To do this, we can use the property of congruences that states the sum of congruent numbers is congruent to the sum of the congruents modulo 11. That is:\n\\[ a + b + c + d \\equiv 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\nFirst, we calculate the sum on the right-hand side:\n\\[ 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20 \\]\nNext, we find the remainder when 20 is divided by 11. We perform the division:\n\\[ 20 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 9 \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ 20 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\nTherefore, the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by 11 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
